.TH QSH_CONFIG 5 "" "qsh" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
qsh_config \- qsh configuration files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/qsh/qsh_config
.br
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qsh/config
.SH DESCRIPTION
This version of
.BR qsh (1)
reads no configuration file. The paths above are reserved for a later version, which will
read a TOML file with a
.B [defaults]
table and
.B [host."pattern"]
tables, the user's file overriding the system's.
.PP
Everything about reaching a host comes from
.BR ssh (1)
and its own configuration
.RI ( ~/.ssh/config ,
.BR ssh_config (5)):
host aliases, users, ports, keys, agents, jump hosts and known hosts work for
.B qsh
exactly as for
.BR ssh .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QSH_TRANSPORTS
Comma-separated transports
.B qsh
may use, of
.BR quic ,
.B tls
and
.BR ssh ;
all three when unset. For diagnosing networks.
.TP
.B QSH_SSH
The ssh program
.B qsh
runs, instead of
.B ssh
from
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B QSH_SERVER_PORTS
The port range
.RI ( FIRST\-LAST )
of a
.BR qsh\-server (1)
daemon started on demand; 60443\-60542 when unset.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/qsh/
.RI ( ~/.local/state/qsh/ )
The daemon's certificate and key
.RI ( daemon/ ),
and the log of a daemon started on demand
.RI ( daemon.log ).
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/qsh/
.RI ( /tmp/qsh\- UID
when unset)
The daemon's control socket and lock; mode 0700, owned by the user.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR qsh (1),
.BR qsh\-server (1),
.BR ssh_config (5)
